julianne smith u.s ambassador to nato  thank you so much uh for spending a few  minutes talking to us today let's kick  off uh with the expansion of nato what's  the reason to expand nato to include  finland and sweden well the nato allies  over the last couple of months have made  it crystal clear particularly to folks  in moscow that its open door policy  remains the same and for that reason  we welcomed the application by finland  and sweden today when they walked in  with their letters stating their intent  to join the alliance these two nations  share our values and they share a  collective commitment to our security  across the euro-atlantic area they bring  very capable militaries they've  exercised with us for many many years in  places like the baltic sea and we're  confident that when they come into the  alliance and become full-fledged members  in the weeks ahead months ahead that  they will make a major contribution to  the nato alliance  ambassador it can take up to a year for  countries to be allowed into the  alliance for that application to be  processed are you saying you want to  fast-track that into weeks or months  yeah so in recent weeks the allies have  come together to try and look at ways  that they can reduce the timelines  surrounding the process there are a  number of steps involved there's  something called the accession protocol  which all allies have to sign here at  nato and the two new members would sign  that as well in order to get to that  signature we'll have to go through  something called the accession talks  once the protocol is actually signed  then every member of the alliance has to  ratify the agreement which can take some  time and you're right in the past we've  seen instances where that process has  taken upwards of a year but given the  situation on the ground in ukraine the  fact that we have a war that's ongoing  on european territory i think all allies  are in agreement that this process needs  to be expedited so ambassador just be  clear you're hoping to get this achieved  in the coming months so what by the  summer by the end of the year  ideally i think we'd like to see it done  in a couple of months that's the goal  right okay i'm just i'll just bring you  straight to something else then  just in terms of that  once those countries apply it is a  moment of vulnerability and risk  the uk foreign secretary today has  congratulated both countries on the  decision and says the uk will work to  integrate them quickly but what will the  u.s do to protect both countries should  russia  attack them before they are in the  alliance what's your plan  well fortunately for finland and sweden  all of the members of the alliance  recognize that in this process between  the moment when the countries actually  sign the assession protocol the  agreement to join the nato alliance  and the period of ratification all the  way to the point when they become  full-fledged members that there is some  risk involved in this process and for  that reason  aspirant countries going through this  process can sometimes feel a bit exposed  and for that reason  all of the allies many of them have been  in conversations directly with sweden  and finland to find ways that they can  take steps to reassure those two  countries and to send a very strong  clear signal back to moscow that all of  us will  address any future forms of aggression  that any country could take towards  sweden and finland during this period so  as you perhaps have seen the white house  has recently announced that both heads  of state from sweden and finland will be  visiting washington on thursday i think  you'll see more in terms of what the u.s  wants to say about this period but  already as you noted the united kingdom  has been quite clear in its commitment  to the security of those two nations as  well ambassador it sounds like there  could be more news on this specifically  from the white house later in the week  but as you said the uk have signed a  bilateral pact  with both countries to protect them  i'm assuming that you're suggesting that  the us is minded to do the same  we're certainly well aware of the risks  involved and we will take steps to make  sure that they feel that their security  needs are being addressed okay  ambassador just to quickly go back to  that accession process obviously the  white house is clearly minded to approve  uh this application anyone can ask to  join but of course the members of nato  have to agree it turkey is a stumbling  block isn't it it's said it is opposed  the white house said this week it's  confident consensus can be reached can  you explain to us what's going on and  why the white house is confident of that  has president biden been in bilateral  talks  well first and foremost let me say yes  there has been an ally turkey that has  come forward to express some concerns  they have specific security concerns  that they've raised with all of the  allies here at nato headquarters and  we're all taking those concerns very  seriously i think the reason you see the  confidence stemming out of the white  house and in fact many other capitals  across europe and here at nato  headquarters is simply because the  strength of the alliance really rests on  our ability to continually bring forward  disagreements debates alternative points  of view and work through those  disagreements to reach consensus  everything that nato does  has to be reached through consensus you  have to have 30 allies vote on it so we  have 73 years of experience of working  through allied differences and for that  reason i too personally am quite  confident that we'll find a way through  this just be clear then you don't think  in the end turkey will be the block  i think we will see eventually sweden  and finland joining the alliance in the  not too distant future ambassador i've  got a few minutes left just a couple  more things uh let's just get back to  russia nato defensive alliance being a  member is designed to be a deterrent  to attacks from aggressors such as  russia but speaking to russian officials  last week they couldn't be clearer  they're clear that joining nato will  make those countries less safe let's  just have a quick listen to what the  russian ambassador to the european union  told me last week as  my government  has  pointed out  this will necessitate certain military  technical measures  like uh im improving  or raising the now the degree  of uh uh defense preparations along the  finnish border and the russian finnish  border is more than 1300 kilometers i  should remind you  ambassador that you not so russia  warning that it will make these  countries less safe they're warning  about technical measures along the  border are you not worried about further  poking the bear that this actually risks  escalation of the uk and conflict well  look russia has already escalated and  fundamentally shifted the security  landscape across the european continent  russia has started a land war in europe  in 2022 the first of its kind since  world war ii so russia is the aggressor  here i think russia's actions have  triggered some new and fresh thinking in  these two countries and triggered their  decision to join the alliance so the  only concern that i think anyone should  have is about russian aggression i know  for a fact that bringing these two  nations into the alliance will make us  all collectively stronger and certainly  it will make finland and sweden stronger  as well no doubt about that just quickly  when russia says this that it will make  these countries less safe are they lying  or are they bluffing or do you not know  well what they're doing in ukraine is  making all of us less safe collectively  and so we want their actions in ukraine  to stop how they respond to this  potential round of nato enlargement is  up to them but i think right now the  message coming across from the nato  alliance is crystal clear and that is  that russia doesn't get a veto over nato  enlargement policy this decision rests  between the nato alliance and finland  and sweden they have taken the decision  to submit that letter of application and  so we will proceed irrespective of any  threats that russia is going to make but  are you worried about the nuclear threat  ambassador russia's lost a third of its  invading force in ukraine surely the  threat that they are uh  warning of is nuclear surely nato must  be concerned about that and has nato  discussed how it might respond to a  russian nuclear attack surely you are  now gaming this as an option  we are worried any time the russians are  talking talking about using nuclear  weapons whether it's tactical nukes or  some other form of nuclear weapons we  find this rhetoric to be extremely  dangerous and troubling and for that  reason yes we do hear at the alliance  look at all potential contingencies any  potential scenario we do not want them  to turn to rely on these types of  weapons nor do we want to see them use  chemical weapons and that's why i think  you've seen some very clear messaging  both from the white house and from nato  allies about the danger and are urging  them to not turn to these types of  weapons so we take it very seriously and  i think we've all messaged back that  that would incur severe consequences  final question ambassador i'm out of  time i don't expect you of course to set  out nato plans explicitly on how you  might respond  but i think what you're saying to me  that is that the alliance has drawn up  or is in the process of drawing up  response options should russia escalate  this in a nuclear fashion  i think what i'm saying is that the nato  alliance has been planning for this war  in ukraine for quite some time before  russia went into ukraine on february  24th we were conducting  all sorts of planning to prepare for  this possible scenario while we were  working on a diplomatic solution as well  now that the war is under away we're  constantly reassessing how individually  we can support ukraine nato is not  active in ukraine or formally providing  any sort of support but of course as an  alliance we're looking at our individual  contributions to strengthen the  ukrainian forces and preparing for every  contingency and we will keep doing so  okay ambassador thank you very much for  your time
 

